      "text": "In the right triangle $ABC$, where $\\angle A = 90^\\circ$, we have $AB$ as one of the legs and $BC$ as the hypotenuse. Given that $AB = 16$ and $BC = 24$, we aim to find $\\sin A$, which is the ratio of the opposite side to the hypotenuse.\n\nStep 1: Identify the opposite side to $\\angle A$.\nSince $\\angle A$ is the right angle, we are looking at side $AB$. Therefore, the side opposite to $\\angle A$ is $AB$.\n\nStep 2: Identify the hypotenuse.\nThe hypothenuse is the side opposite the right angle, which is $BC$ in this case.\n\nStep 3: Calculate the ratio of the opposite side to the hypotenuse.\nThe sine of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the side opposite the angle to the length of the hypotenuse. So, for $\\angle A$:\n\\[\n\\sin A = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\frac{AB}{BC} = \\frac{16}{24}\n\\]\n\nStep 4: Simplify the fraction.\nTo simplify $\\frac{16}{24}$, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by their greatest common divisor, which is 8:\n\\[\n\\sin A = \\frac{16 \\div 8}{24 \\div 8} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of $\\sin A$ is $\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}$.",
