      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find \\(\\sin A\\) in the right triangle \\(ABC\\) with \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), we need to identify the lengths of the sides relative to \\(\\angle A\\) (which is the right angle in this case). However, the given sides \\(AB = 16\\) and \\(BC = 24\\) do not represent the opposite and adjacent sides for \\(\\angle A\\) in the standard way because \\(AB\\) and \\(BC\\) are the legs of the right triangle and not the opposite and adjacent sides corresponding to \\(\\angle A\\).\n\nLet's assume the correct interpretation is that \\(AB\\) is the adjacent side to \\(\\angle A\\) and \\(BC\\) is the hypotenuse. Here, \\(AB = 16\\) and \\(BC = 24\\) are the two legs of the right triangle, and \\(AC\\) is the adjacent side to \\(\\angle A\\) as well. We need to find the length of \\(AC\\) first.\n\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem, which states:\n\\[\nAB^2 + AC^2 = BC^2\n\\]\nwe substitute the given values:\n\\[\n16^2 + AC^2 = 24^2\n\\]\n\\[\n256 + AC^2 = 576\n\\]\nSolving for \\(AC^2\\):\n\\[\nAC^2 = 576 - 256\n\\]\n\\[\nAC^2 = 320\n\\]\n\\[\nAC = \\sqrt{320} = \\sqrt{64 \\times 5} = 8\\sqrt{5}\n\\]\n\nNow, we can find \\(\\sin A\\). In a right triangle, \\(\\sin A\\) is the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the hypotenuse. Here, the opposite side to \\(\\angle A\\) is \\(AB\\) and the hypotenuse is \\(BC\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\sin A = \\frac{AB}{BC} = \\frac{16}{24} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\sin A\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\n\\]",
